Packet-Level Verification
 Captured Wireshark trace confirmed:

  . No AS-REQ/AS-REP — the AS exchange was bypassed entirely (ticket forged offline)
  . TGS-REQ/TGS-REP present — DC accepted the forged TGT and issued a real CIFS service ticket
  . Service: cifs/WIN-56JEHJD0RI3.redteamlab.local
  . A clean TGS-REP (no KRB-ERROR) proves the DC cryptographically validated the forged TGT

Critical Lesson: IP vs Hostname
Targeting by IP forces NTLM fallback regardless of ticket cache —
 always use FQDN hostname matching the SPN for Kerberos tools.

Remediation:
 . Rotate krbtgt password twice with a gap between rotations (AD retains previous key temporarily for
   in-flight ticket grace)
 . A single rotation leaves the prior key valid during the grace period

Interview Explanation:
"A Golden Ticket is a forged Kerberos TGT signed with the domain's krbtgt key.
Because Kerberos trust is stateless — the KDC trusts any TGT that decrypts correctly with its own key,
without checking whether it ever issued it — an attacker who steals krbtgt's hash can forge tickets for any
identity with any group membership. I obtained the hash via DCSync, forged the ticket using impacket-ticketer,
and verified acceptance at the packet level by confirming a clean TGS-REP with no AS-REQ/AS-REP exchange."

  

